---
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro'
import Preview from '~/components/Preview.astro'
import PropsTable from '~/components/PropsTable.astro'
import FormFields from '~/demos/form-fields'
import FormValidation from '~/demos/form-validation'
import ComboboxDemo from '~/demos/combobox-demo'
---

<DocsShell
  title="Forms"
  description="Fields, controls and the wiring that keeps a label attached to its input."
>
  <h2 id="field">Field</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Field</code> generates the ids and the <code>aria-describedby</code> /
    <code>aria-invalid</code> relationships between a label, a control, its description and its error.
    That is the part that is easy to get subtly wrong by hand; everything visual is ours.
  </p>

  <Preview demo="form-fields" title="The controls" contained={false}>
    <FormFields client:visible />
  </Preview>

  <Preview
    demo="form-validation"
    title="Validation"
    description="Errors appear only once the field has been touched, so a pristine form is not covered in red."
  >
    <FormValidation client:visible />
  </Preview>

  <h3>Accessibility</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The required asterisk on <code>Field.Label</code> is decorative. Put <code>required</code> on the
      control too — that is what actually gets announced.
    </li>
    <li>
      Controls in a group name themselves from their own label, so a radio group announces "Light",
      not the field's name three times.
    </li>
    <li>Every control is reachable and operable with the keyboard alone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>API</h3>
  <PropsTable type="FieldProps" element="div" />
  <PropsTable type="InputProps" element="input" />

  <h2 id="combobox">Combobox</h2>
  <p>
    A filterable select. Pass the full collection to <code>items</code> on the root — that is what makes
    filtering, typeahead and virtual focus work — and render the list with a function child.
  </p>
  <Preview demo="combobox-demo" title="Filtering">
    <ComboboxDemo client:visible />
  </Preview>
  <PropsTable type="ComboboxInputProps" element="input" />

  <h2 id="select">Select</h2>
  <p>
    Also pass <code>items</code> to <code>Select</code>: it is what lets the trigger render the
    selected item's <em>label</em> rather than its raw value.
  </p>
  <PropsTable type="SelectTriggerProps" element="button" />
</DocsShell>
